==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{wp}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*skey-}}
A parallel form of {{m|en|sheave}}, from {{inh|en|enm|schyve}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*skībā}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*skībǭ}}, presumably through an {{inh|en|ang|*sċīfe}} (though it is not attested before the Middle English period). Cognate with {{cog|de|Scheibe}}, late {{cog|non|skífa||slice}}, {{m|non|brauðskífa||slice of bread}} (whence {{cog|da|skive||disc, slice}}), {{cog|nl|schijf||disc, slice}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ʃaɪv/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-shive.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪv|s=1}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[slice]], especially of bread.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus|2|1|passage=Of a cut loafe to steale a '''shiue'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|author=Anthony Burgess|title=Earthly Powers
|passage=In my cool room with the shutters shut and the thin '''shives''' of air and light coming through the slats, I cried myself to sleep in an overloud selfpitying transport.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[sheave]].
# A [[beam]] or [[plank]] of split wood.
# A flat, wide [[cork]] for plugging a large hole or closing a wide-mouthed bottle.

